\begin_layout Standard
To calculate the standard error for a dataset containing 
\begin_inset Formula $\{-1,+1\}$
\end_inset

 values the formula changes slightly from the standard 
\begin_inset Formula $\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

 binary format because the variance of the data is 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\bar{x}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For your specific subset of size 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1270$
\end_inset

 with a sample mean of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}=-0.8$
\end_inset

,
 the standard error (SE) is 
\begin_inset Formula $0.0168$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Step-by-Step Calculation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Calculate the Sample Variance:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For any dataset containing only 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

,
 the variance simplifies perfectly to 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\bar{x}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\text{Variance} & = & 1-(-0.8)^{2}=1-0.64=0.36
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 Divide by Sample Size (
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Divide this variance by your total number of observations.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{0.36}{1270} & \approx & 0.00028346
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.
 Take the Square Root:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The square root of this value gives you the Standard Error.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
SE & = & \sqrt{0.00028346}\approx\mathbf{0.0168}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Key Statistical Insight & Anomaly
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a major statistical anomaly between your sample mean and your expected value:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Proportion:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 A mean of 
\begin_inset Formula $-0.8$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $10\%$
\end_inset

 of your data points are 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $90\%$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Expectation:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 If the true theoretical expectation value is exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 it means 
\begin_inset Formula $100\%$
\end_inset

 of the population must be 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

.
 If the population expectation is 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

,
 the true variance is 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 meaning it is mathematically impossible to sample a 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Z-Score:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Testing how far your sample mean (
\begin_inset Formula $-0.8$
\end_inset

) is from the expectation (
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

) yields an enormous Z-score:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Z & = & \frac{-0.8-(-1)}{0.0168}\approx11.9
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A Z-score of nearly 
\begin_inset Formula $12$
\end_inset

 means that your sample mean is 12 standard errors away from the expected value.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This indicates that either your sample is a structural departure from the expected model or that the theoretical expectation of 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 does not apply to this system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How to proceed
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you want to evaluate the other three subsets,
 let me know:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* What are their sample sizes (
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

)?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* What are their sample means (
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

)?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Are these datasets part of a physics/spin model experiment (like an Ising model)?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I can calculate the standard errors for the remaining sets and help you interpret the variance!
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
n = 1270,
 mean = -0.8
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Variance for +1/-1 data:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Var(X) & = & E[X^{2}]-(E[X])^{2}\\
 & = & 1-mean^{2}variance\\
 & = & 1-(mean**2)se\\
 & = & \sqrt{variance/n}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
print(f"Variance:
 {variance}")
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
print(f"SE:
 {se}")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Hypothesis Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To run an hypothesis test on this dataset it is best to shift from the 
\begin_inset Formula $\{-1,+1\}$
\end_inset

 scale to a proportion scale treating the 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 values as "errors" or "invalid data".
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the sample mean is 
\begin_inset Formula $-0.8$
\end_inset

 your sample proportion of invalid data (
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{p}$
\end_inset

) is exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $10\%$
\end_inset

 (or 
\begin_inset Formula $0.10$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Your sample size (
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

) is 1270.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here is how to set up and execute the hypothesis test depending on what you want to prove.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Scenario A:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Testing against the perfect theoretical model (0% errors):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To test whether or not that data perfectly matches the expectation value of 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 (meaning a 
\begin_inset Formula $0\%$
\end_inset

 error rate) a standard Z-test fails because a population with zero variance yields a division-by-zero error.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead use an Exact Binomial Test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Null Hypothesis (
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

):
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The true error rate is 
\begin_inset Formula $0\%$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $p=0$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Alternative Hypothesis (
\begin_inset Formula $H_{a}$
\end_inset

):
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The true error rate is greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $0\%$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $p>0$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Math:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 the probability of observing even a single 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 is exactly zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Conclusion:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Because 127 invalid data points (
\begin_inset Formula $10\%$
\end_inset

 of 1270) were observed the 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-value is strictly 0.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Therefore the null hypothesis must be rejected with absolute certainty as the process is generating a statistically significant number of errors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Scenario B:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Test against an acceptable error threshold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In real-world systems a 
\begin_inset Formula $0\%$
\end_inset

 error rate is often impossible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead,
 test whether the 
\begin_inset Formula $10\%$
\end_inset

 error rate is significantly worse than an "acceptable" baseline or equipment tolerance (for example,
 a maximum allowable error rate of 
\begin_inset Formula $5\%$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}=0.05$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To perform a One-Sample Z-Test for Proportions using a $5
\backslash
%$ baseline:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
State the Hypotheses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Null Hypothesis (
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

):
 
\begin_inset Formula $p=0.05$
\end_inset

 (The error rate is within the acceptable 
\begin_inset Formula $5\%$
\end_inset

 limit).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Alternative Hypothesis (
\begin_inset Formula $H_{a}$
\end_inset

):
 
\begin_inset Formula $p>0.05$
\end_inset

 (The error rate significantly exceeds the 
\begin_inset Formula $5\%$
\end_inset

 limit).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Calculate the Standard Error under the Null Hypothesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use the baseline value (
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}=0.05$
\end_inset

) to calculate the expected standard error:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
SE_{0} & = & \sqrt{\frac{p_{0}(1-p_{0})}{n}}=\sqrt{\frac{0.05\times0.95}{1270}}=\sqrt{\frac{0.0475}{1270}}\approx0.00612
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Calculate the Test Statistic (Z-score)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Determine how many standard errors the sample proportion (
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{p}=0.10$
\end_inset

) sits away from the baseline (
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}=0.05$
\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Z & = & \frac{\hat{p}-p_{0}}{SE_{0}}=\frac{0.10-0.05}{0.00612}\approx\mathbf{8.17}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Determine the 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-value and Conclude
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A Z-score of 
\begin_inset Formula $8.17$
\end_inset

 is exceptionally high.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For context,
 any Z-score above 
\begin_inset Formula $1.96$
\end_inset

 is statistically significant at the 
\begin_inset Formula $95\%$
\end_inset

 confidence level.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-value here is practically zero (
\begin_inset Formula $p<0.00001$
\end_inset

),
 meaning that the null hypothesis should be rejected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\begin_inset Formula $10\%$
\end_inset

 error rate is overwhelmingly higher than the 
\begin_inset Formula $5\%$
\end_inset

 threshold,
 ruling out random chance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
How to proceed
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To tailor this test precisely to your research let me know:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Do you have a specific acceptable error threshold (like 
\begin_inset Formula $1\%$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $5\%$
\end_inset

,
 etc.) in mind for your system?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Would you like to see how to calculate a Confidence Interval to find the upper and lower bounds of your true error rate?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Do you want to compare two of your subsets to see if one has a significantly higher error rate than the other?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Comment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is incredibly common in physics and data science to find that real-world experimental data is messy compared to pure theoretical models.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the expectation value is 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 even a tiny amount of experimental noise,
 instrument misalignment or thermal fluctuations will flip some 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 values to 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 creating that 
\begin_inset Formula $10\%$
\end_inset

 "noise" floor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because a standard hypothesis test cannot be used against a perfect 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 expectation (as it assumes zero noise) a Confidence Interval (CI) or a Chi-Square Goodness-of-Fit Test can be used to rigorously benchmark this experimental data against theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Method 1:
 Quantify the Noise with a Confidence Interval
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead of trying to prove the data perfectly matches 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 you can use your data to state exactly how noisy the experiment is with 95% confidence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Calculate the Error Margin:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using your previously calculated standard error (
\begin_inset Formula $SE=0.0168$
\end_inset

) and a standard 95% confidence Z-score (
\begin_inset Formula $1.96$
\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\text{Margin of Error} & = & 1.96\times0.0168\approx0.033
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 Establish the Bounds:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Apply this to your sample mean of 
\begin_inset Formula $-0.8$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\text{95\% Confidence Interval} & = & [-0.833,-0.767]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This means that it can be confidently stated in your paper that this specific experimental setup yields an expectation value between 
\begin_inset Formula $-0.833$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-0.767$
\end_inset

.
 This formally establishes that the real-world dataset operates with a 
\begin_inset Formula $10\%\pm1.6\%$
\end_inset

 systemic noise/error rate relative to the theoretical ideal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Method 2:
 Chi-Square Test with an "Acceptable Noise" Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you know from literature that this specific experimental equipment typically has a certain baseline noise floor (e.g.,
 a 
\begin_inset Formula $5\%$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $8\%$
\end_inset

 rate of random bit-flips/falses),
 you can test if this dataset fits that noisy theoretical expectation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example,
 if your theory predicts a baseline but you modify it to account for an expected 
\begin_inset Formula $8\%$
\end_inset

 experimental noise floor:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Your Theoretical Expected Counts:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

1,168 counts of 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 and 102 counts of 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Actual Observed Counts:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 1,143 counts of 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 and 127 counts of 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then run a quick Chi-Square calculation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\chi^{2} & = & \sum\frac{(\text{Observed}-\text{Expected})^{2}}{\text{Expected}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the resulting 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-value is greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $0.05$
\end_inset

 then you can formally state that the data does match your theoretical model once the known experimental noise floor is factored in.
\end_layout

